{"id":"laser-grid","title":"Laser Grid","url":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Laser_Grid","rev":238825,"fetched":"2026-10-16","lead":[{"t":"p","c":[{"t":"b","c":["Laser Grid"]}," is a visual design aesthetic that was prevalent in the 1980s, overlapping with ",{"t":"a","c":["Memphis Design"],"id":"memphis-design"}," and ",{"t":"a","c":["Memphis Lite"],"id":"memphis-lite"},". It is characterized by laser-like visuals on dark backgrounds, a grid motif, and airbrushed chrome effects. In architecture and interior design, right-angle shapes and a combination of black with reflective surfaces such as chrome and glass are typical."]},{"t":"p","c":["This aesthetic happens to be the source of the 1980s love of glass block walls and floor-to-ceiling mirrors, such as those found on closet sliding doors. Laser Grid is often used to retroactively depict the 1980s, particularly in ",{"t":"a","c":["Synthwave"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Synthwave"}," media."]}],"sections":[{"title":"History","id":"history","blocks":[{"t":"p","c":["Early vector computer graphics were not meant to be recorded or transferred to film; indeed, they used special screens. Nonetheless, they were used as early as 1972 to create animation. From then on, and into the early 1980s, they were commonly featured on computer screens in various movies from ",{"t":"i","c":["Star Wars"]}," (1977) to ",{"t":"i","c":["2001: A Space Odyssey"]}," (1968). The Grid constituted a major set design element in 1981's ",{"t":"i","c":["Looker"]},", but the two movies that most imprinted it as a futuristic design on the popular psyche were 1979's ",{"t":"i","c":["Black Hole"]}," and 1982's ",{"t":"i","c":["Tron"]},"."]},{"t":"p","c":["Laser Grid's popularity was a consequence of how convenient and easy it was to draw as a shorthand for technology. This, and the recent development of actual laser technology at the time, also helps to explain its popularity amongst designers, for whom it is immediately reminiscent of gridded cutting mats. Despite the name and its sources, computers would not come to be central in graphic design until well into the 1990s, and these grids continued to be drawn by hand even as they faded away into memory. The persistent use of vector graphics throughout the mid and late 1980s is surprising, as by then ",{"t":"a","c":["8-Bit"],"id":"8-bit"}," games had fully supplanted vector consoles."]},{"t":"p","c":["As the Laser Grid (sometimes in a more vector-like presentation rather than a geometric grid) spread within the Technosphere, it merged with the heavily airbrushed style ubiquitous in advertising at the time to create a visual that went heavy on chrome looks. The grid pattern then spread, in a limited fashion, to interior design creating a sleek, almost monochrome look which was applied to reflective surfaces such as chrome, mirrors and glass blocks. The popularity of Art Deco revival at the time (",{"t":"a","c":["Deco-Luxe"],"href":"https://cari.institute/aesthetics/deco-luxe"},") further contributed to reinforce chrome's popularity in interior design. As a result, large mirrored surfaces became widespread both inside and outside buildings, cladding the entirety of certain office structures."]},{"t":"p","c":["In logo design, many companies took inspiration from the striped logo of IBM, which was redesigned in 1962, causing the stripe motif to become pervasive in the late 1970s and into the 1980s: AT&T, Microsoft, Sierra Entertainment (to name a few), as well as the logos for ",{"t":"i","c":["Input"]}," and ",{"t":"i","c":["PC Magazine"]},"."]},{"t":"p","c":["Although it was largely limited to the Technosphere (and even then did not necessarily dominate the advertising language), Laser Grid visuals became a key part of Retrowave aesthetics like ",{"t":"a","c":["Synthwave"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Synthwave"}," and, to a lesser degree, ",{"t":"a","c":["Vaporwave"],"href":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Vaporwave"},". Less commonly, the grid motif also appears in ",{"t":"a","c":["Memphis Design"],"id":"memphis-design"}," and ",{"t":"a","c":["Memphis Lite"],"id":"memphis-lite"}," media."]}]},{"title":"Visuals","id":"visuals","blocks":[{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":["Grids, either in perspective or flat"]},{"c":["Wireframes"]},{"c":["Manually drawn vector graphics in light on dark background"]},{"c":["Pinstriped logos and text"]},{"c":["Glowing lines and other elements"]},{"c":["Lasers"]},{"c":["Neon lighting as a structuring or decorating element"]},{"c":["Heavy use of airbrush, especially to create a glowing or chromed appearance"]},{"c":["Glass blocks"]},{"c":["Chrome and glass combo"]},{"c":["Large reflective surfaces: for example, chrome slat ceilings"]}]}]},{"title":"Media","id":"media","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Films"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["The Andromeda Strain"]}," (1971)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["A Computer Animated Hand"]}," (1972)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Star Wars"]}," (1977)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["The Black Hole"]}," (1979)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Adam Powers, The Juggler"]}," (1981)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Looker"]}," (1981)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Timerider: The Adventure of Lyle Swann"]}," (1982)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Tron"]}," (1982)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["TV"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Airwolf"]}," (1984)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Doctor Who"]}," (John Nathan-Turner era, 1980-1989)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["He-Man and the Masters of the Universe"]}," (1983-1985)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["M.A.S.K."]}," (1985)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["The Transformers"]}," (1984)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["WWF Golden Era"]}," (1984-1993)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Captain Power and the Soldiers of the Future"]}," (1987)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Video Games"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Arcade Neon Golf"]}," (2015)"]},{"c":[{"t":"i","c":["Synthriders"]}," (2018)"]}]},{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Other"]},{"t":"ul","items":[{"c":[{"t":"a","c":["National Theatre Corp. Policy Trailer"],"href":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WzSiqJj3HdM"}," (1985)"]},{"c":["\"",{"t":"a","c":["Atomic Dog"],"href":"https://youtu.be/LuyS9M8T03A"},"\" by George Clinton (1982)"]}]}]},{"title":"Gallery","id":"gallery","blocks":[{"t":"h","l":3,"c":["Visual Design"]},{"t":"gallery","items":[{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/33/Consider_Her_Ways_And_Others_%2883%29.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20240526172658","w":185,"h":185,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Consider Her Ways And Others"]},", Penguin Books (1979)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/2/2f/The_Black_Hole_Trailer_1979/revision/latest?cb=20250524171047","w":185,"h":185,"v":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/File:The_Black_Hole_Trailer_1979","cap":["Trailer for ",{"t":"i","c":["The Black Hole"]}," (1972)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/3d/RCA_Selectavision_650_VCR_commercial_1980/revision/latest?cb=20240526185316","w":185,"h":185,"v":"https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/File:RCA_Selectavision_650_VCR_commercial_1980"},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/8/83/Tron-disneyscreencaps-com-7858.png/revision/latest?cb=20250524171725","w":185,"h":185,"cap":[{"t":"i","c":["Tron"]}," (1982), probably the ur-example of the aesthetic"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/9/95/Educational_Software_for_the_PC%2C_1982.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20240526172658","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Educational Software for the PC ad, ",{"t":"i","c":["PC Mag"]}," (1982)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/a/a1/R-11598969-1519924133-2027.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20250513035420","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["André Cymone, ",{"t":"i","c":["Livin' In The New Wave"]}," (1982)"]},{"src":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/7/7e/Non-grid_laser_grid_game_art_%28Epyx_Games%29.jpg/revision/latest?cb=20240526172700","w":185,"h":185,"cap":["Laser/Vector design of 80s Epyx Games ",{"t"